limited volume vials there's three kinds  Max recovery insert vials and  polypropylene insert vials or plastic  limited volume vials are imperative to  use when you have a limited amount of  sample a limited volume vial raises the  height of the sample in the vial so your  auto sampler can pick up the sample more  easily the two most important  specifications to focus on are residual  volume which is how much volume is lost  in the vial and total volume which is  how much sample volume can be put in the  vial next Max recovery vials are  designed with a conical bottom for the  best sample recovery these allow for  high sample volume and low residual  volume it also minimizes  cross-contamination by eliminating the  need to transfer the sample to micro  volume inserts limited volume vials have  low residual volume but hold low sample  volume polypropylene vials are similar  to limited vials but they are plastic  for applications that need them they are  also very cost effective when you see a  descriptor like 9 to 425 the first  number is the diameter nine millimeters  and the second number is the thread size  425. choose amber glass if your analyte  is light sensitive and clear glass if  it's not
 

